@host = http://localhost:5080

### Health
GET {{host}}/health

### The status graph the UI drives its buttons from
GET {{host}}/api/orders/statuses

### The next reference, as the intake form fetches it. Call it twice and you get
### two different numbers — it is a counter, not a reservation, and nothing is
### held. The requests below deliberately ignore it and bring their own reference,
### which the API accepts: that is the integration path the form does not use.
GET {{host}}/api/orders/next-reference

### Submit an order. Expect 201 Created, X-Idempotent-Replay: false
# @name submit
POST {{host}}/api/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "externalReference": "PO-DEMO-1",
  "currency": "USD",
  "notes": "Recorded from the .http file.",
  "customer": { "email": "sam@contoso.com", "name": "Sam Carter" },
  "lines": [
    { "sku": "KB-001", "name": "Mechanical Keyboard", "quantity": 2, "unitPrice": 89.99 },
    { "sku": "MS-014", "name": "Wireless Mouse",      "quantity": 3, "unitPrice": 24.50 }
  ]
}

### THE DEMO: send exactly the same thing again.
### Expect 200 OK, X-Idempotent-Replay: true, identical id. Still one order.
POST {{host}}/api/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "externalReference": "PO-DEMO-1",
  "currency": "USD",
  "notes": "A different note. Cosmetic, so still the same order.",
  "customer": { "email": "sam@contoso.com", "name": "Sam Carter" },
  "lines": [
    { "sku": "MS-014", "name": "Wireless Mouse",      "quantity": 3, "unitPrice": 24.50 },
    { "sku": "KB-001", "name": "Mechanical Keyboard", "quantity": 2, "unitPrice": 89.99 }
  ]
}

### Same reference, different quantity. That is a different order wearing a
### borrowed reference. Expect 409 REFERENCE_REUSED_WITH_DIFFERENT_CONTENT.
POST {{host}}/api/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "externalReference": "PO-DEMO-1",
  "currency": "USD",
  "customer": { "email": "sam@contoso.com", "name": "Sam Carter" },
  "lines": [
    { "sku": "KB-001", "name": "Mechanical Keyboard", "quantity": 99, "unitPrice": 89.99 }
  ]
}

### Same reference, different customer. Legitimately a different order.
### Expect 201 Created.
POST {{host}}/api/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "externalReference": "PO-DEMO-1",
  "currency": "USD",
  "customer": { "email": "other@contoso.com", "name": "Other Customer" },
  "lines": [
    { "sku": "KB-001", "name": "Mechanical Keyboard", "quantity": 1, "unitPrice": 89.99 }
  ]
}

### Validation: negative price, zero quantity, bad currency, repeated SKU.
### Expect 400 with every problem listed at once.
POST {{host}}/api/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "externalReference": "",
  "currency": "DOLLARS",
  "customer": { "email": "not-an-email", "name": "" },
  "lines": [
    { "sku": "A", "name": "Thing", "quantity": 0,  "unitPrice": -5 },
    { "sku": "A", "name": "Thing", "quantity": 1,  "unitPrice": 1.005 }
  ]
}

### List, newest first
GET {{host}}/api/orders?page=1&pageSize=20

### Server-side filtering
GET {{host}}/api/orders?status=Pending&search=sam

### Fetch one order
GET {{host}}/api/orders/{{submit.response.body.id}}

### Confirm it. Expect 200, status Confirmed.
PUT {{host}}/api/orders/{{submit.response.body.id}}/status
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "Confirmed" }

### Confirm it again. Already in that status, so 200 with X-Status-Changed: false.
PUT {{host}}/api/orders/{{submit.response.body.id}}/status
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "Confirmed" }

### Fulfil it.
PUT {{host}}/api/orders/{{submit.response.body.id}}/status
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "Fulfilled" }

### Now try to cancel a fulfilled order. Expect 409 with allowedTransitions: [].
PUT {{host}}/api/orders/{{submit.response.body.id}}/status
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "Cancelled" }
